      <section class="py-5 border-bottom-new-design">
        <div class="container">
          <h2 class="text-center mb-5">Technology embedded in Polkadot Development</h2>
          <div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-lg-2 g-4">
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Substrate</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">The substrate is a framework for building blockchains that powers
                    Polkadot’s relay chain. It also includes Parity Technologies' learnings about building Ethereum,
                    Bitcoin and enterprise blockchains.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Web Assembly</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">Web Assembly (Wasm), an extremely performant virtual environment
                    that compiles the Polkadot state machine Wasm, was created by tech giants such as Google, Apple
                    and Microsoft.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">libp2p</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">Polkadot uses libp2p to network, which is a flexible
                    platform-to-platform network framework that allows peer-to-peer applications. Libp2p is the
                    platform for future decentralized applications and handles peer discovery and communication within
                    the Polkadot ecosystem.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Runtime Environment</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">The Polkadot runtime environment has been coded in Rust and C++.
                    This makes Polkadot easily accessible to a broad range of developers.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Security model</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">Polkadot allows multiple blockchains to pool security. Instead of
                    using the usual proof-of-work and proof-of-stake systems, the security provided by all blockchains
                    is combined and applied to them all.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Algorithm</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">Polkadot uses the consensus algorithm "GRANDPA", which is a
                    GHOST-based Recursive Ancestor Deriving Prefix Agreement, to build resilient network capabilities.
                    This algorithm includes adaptive mechanisms that allow for instant block finalization..</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="py-5 border-bottom-new-design">
        <div class="container">
          <h2 class="text-center mb-5">Polkadot neural schema</h2>
          <div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-lg-2 g-4">
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Relay chain</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">Polkadot depends on this centralized network that provides network
                    security, consensus and cross-chain interoperability. This chain is made up of many small
                    transactions and does not support smart contracts.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Parachain</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">Para chains are data structures that are specific to an
                    application and validated by the Relay Chain's validators. It allows parallel transactions to
                    ensure system scalability and connects with the relay chain.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Parathreads</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">These parathreads have the ability to send messages to parathreads
                    and parachains via XCMP. This is protected under Polkadot’s validators. Parathreads require a
                    fixed registration fee, which is lower than the cost of purchasing a parachain slot.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Bridges</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">Bridges are special blockchains that connect external networks via
                    shards. These shards allow for parallel processing of data from the outside network, which
                    increases throughput and decreases processing time.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>


          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="py-5 border-bottom-new-design">
